Translation patch: <Straus as VartimeMultiscalarMul>::optional_multiscalar_mul (src/backend/serial/scalar_mul/straus.rs)

Change: The `nafs` and `lookup_tables` collections become nested helpers (`while let` over `next()`;
`lookup_tables` returns `None` at the first `None` point, and the caller returns `None`
instead of using `?`); the `nafs.iter().zip(lookup_tables.iter())` loop becomes an index
loop over `0..min(nafs.len(), lookup_tables.len())`.

Why: the Aeneas Lean library models only `Iterator::next`; the provided methods
(`map`, `zip`, `fold`, `filter`, `collect`, `rev`, `skip`, `size_hint`, `by_ref`, ...)
are missing (AeneasVerif/aeneas#1057), so the original does not build in Lean.
Aeneas also cannot extract a loop that calls `next()` directly on the `IntoIter` of a
generic `IntoIterator` parameter ("Could not find: type_var_id .. from ExtractBase.Item"),
so the consuming loop lives in a nested helper function generic over `It: Iterator`,
called with `x.into_iter()`.
`?` on `Option` is also not modelled.

Behaviour: identical. Scalars are consumed fully first (one `borrow` and one
`non_adjacent_form(5)` per item), then points, polling stopping right after the first
`None`, exactly like `.map(|o| o.map(f)).collect::<Option<Vec<_>>>()?`. The index loop
visits the same pairs as the `zip` in the same order.

Facts relied on (not visible in the diff): `non_adjacent_form(5)` returns `[i8; 256]`, so `nafs[k][i]` with `i < 256` is in bounds.
`add_naf_digit!(acc, naf, table)` (src/backend/util.rs) evaluates `naf`
once and does `acc = &acc + &table.select(d)` / `- &table.select(-d)` / nothing according to
the sign of `d`, so passing `lookup_tables[k]` instead of the reference `lookup_table` is
the same `select` call.

Remove this patch when Aeneas translates the original code: delete the file,
run ./scripts/aeneas-translate.sh and `lake build`, and check both succeed.

diff --git a/curve25519-dalek/src/backend/serial/scalar_mul/straus.rs b/curve25519-dalek/src/backend/serial/scalar_mul/straus.rs
index a20e393..473ed9b 100644
--- a/curve25519-dalek/src/backend/serial/scalar_mul/straus.rs
+++ b/curve25519-dalek/src/backend/serial/scalar_mul/straus.rs
@@ -150,46 +150,72 @@ impl VartimeMultiscalarMul for Straus {
     /// Variable-time Straus using a non-adjacent form of width \\(5\\).
     ///
     /// This is completely similar to the constant-time code, but we
     /// use a non-adjacent form for the scalar, and do not do table
     /// lookups in constant time.
     ///
     /// The non-adjacent form has signed, odd digits.  Using only odd
     /// digits halves the table size (since we only need odd
     /// multiples), or gives fewer additions for the same table size.
     fn optional_multiscalar_mul<I, J>(scalars: I, points: J) -> Option<EdwardsPoint>
     where
         I: IntoIterator,
         I::Item: Borrow<Scalar>,
         J: IntoIterator<Item = Option<EdwardsPoint>>,
     {
         use crate::backend::serial::curve_models::{
             CompletedPoint, ProjectiveNielsPoint, ProjectivePoint,
         };
         use crate::traits::Identity;
         use crate::window::NafLookupTable5;
 
-        let nafs: Vec<_> = scalars
-            .into_iter()
-            .map(|c| c.borrow().non_adjacent_form(5))
-            .collect();
+        fn nafs<It>(mut scalars: It) -> Vec<[i8; 256]>
+        where
+            It: Iterator,
+            It::Item: Borrow<Scalar>,
+        {
+            let mut out = Vec::new();
+            while let Some(c) = scalars.next() {
+                out.push(c.borrow().non_adjacent_form(5));
+            }
+            out
+        }
 
-        let lookup_tables = points
-            .into_iter()
-            .map(|P_opt| P_opt.map(|P| NafLookupTable5::<ProjectiveNielsPoint>::from(&P)))
-            .collect::<Option<Vec<_>>>()?;
+        fn lookup_tables<It>(mut points: It) -> Option<Vec<NafLookupTable5<ProjectiveNielsPoint>>>
+        where
+            It: Iterator<Item = Option<EdwardsPoint>>,
+        {
+            let mut out = Vec::new();
+            while let Some(P_opt) = points.next() {
+                match P_opt {
+                    Some(P) => out.push(NafLookupTable5::<ProjectiveNielsPoint>::from(&P)),
+                    None => return None,
+                }
+            }
+            Some(out)
+        }
+
+        let nafs = nafs(scalars.into_iter());
+
+        let lookup_tables = match lookup_tables(points.into_iter()) {
+            Some(lookup_tables) => lookup_tables,
+            None => return None,
+        };
 
         let mut r = ProjectivePoint::identity();
 
         for i in (0..256).rev() {
             let mut t: CompletedPoint = r.double();
 
-            for (naf, lookup_table) in nafs.iter().zip(lookup_tables.iter()) {
-                add_naf_digit!(t.as_extended(), naf[i], lookup_table);
+            let n = core::cmp::min(nafs.len(), lookup_tables.len());
+            let mut k = 0;
+            while k < n {
+                add_naf_digit!(t.as_extended(), nafs[k][i], lookup_tables[k]);
+                k += 1;
             }
 
             r = t.as_projective();
         }
 
         Some(r.as_extended())
     }
 }
